`-preview=nosharedaccess` now checks conditions and operands

Reading a `shared` lvalue as a condition, or as an operand of an arithmetic,
bitwise, relational or identity expression, used to slip past
`-preview=nosharedaccess`. The same read was rejected when written as an
initializer such as `bool b = c;`. These reads are now rejected as well:

---
shared bool c;
shared int x;

void main()
{
    if (c) {}            // Error: direct access to shared `c` is not allowed
    while (c) {}         // ditto, also `for`, `do`, `assert`, `?:`, `!`, `&&`, `||`
    auto a = x > 0;      // Error: direct access to shared `x` is not allowed
    auto b = x + 1;      // ditto, also `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `^^`, `&`, `|`, `^`, `is`
    int y;
    y += x;              // ditto
    switch (x) { default: } // ditto
    int[] arr;
    auto e = arr[x];     // ditto
}
---

Use `core.atomic` or an explicit cast instead:

---
import core.atomic;

if (atomicLoad(c)) {}
auto a = atomicLoad(x) > 0;
auto b = cast() x + 1;
---

The result of arithmetic on `shared` values that may be accessed directly,
such as fields reached through `this` in a `shared` member function, is an
rvalue and is no longer reported as a shared access itself:

---
struct Pages
{
    size_t numPages, pageSize;

    size_t total() shared
    {
        size_t n = numPages * pageSize; // previously an error, now accepted
        return n;
    }
}
---
